<PublicationLayout
  category="Engineering"
  type="Implementation note"
  title="Tracing a paired-t calculation from observations to p-value"
  description="We connected paired observations to a p-value in one reviewed execution trace; later work closed its two numerical error ledgers and added a reviewed interval trace."
  published="August 31, 2026"
  updated="September 1, 2026"
  status="Reviewed p-value and interval execution traces; interval truth remains open — not protocol support"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        nomue Protocol now has a reviewed, non-authoritative candidate that traces a
        paired-t calculation from explicit paired observations through the test
        statistic and into the two-sided Student-t p-value calculation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The composition candidate was merged after independent adversarial review, and
        its reviewed state is now recorded in the Release 2 candidate readiness files.
        This is not paired-t protocol support. The candidate remains unissued, public
        review is open, and the platform, supported-domain, and runtime decisions remain
        incomplete.
      </p>
      <p>
        Subsequent reviewed work closed the separate mathematical-truth requirements for
        the paired arithmetic and Student-t p-value path. It also added a reviewed
        fixed-95% confidence-interval execution trace. The interval endpoint truth
        requirement remains open, so M3 and Release 2 D5 are not complete.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why-this-step">
    <h2>Why this step was needed</h2>
    <p>
      Earlier work had already produced two separately reviewed execution traces. The
      first starts from paired observations and records the binary64 operations used to
      calculate the paired differences, mean difference, sample variance, standard
      error, test statistic, and integer degrees of freedom. The second starts from a
      test statistic and degrees of freedom and records the Student-t tail calculation
      that produces a p-value.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reviewing those two traces separately does not prove that they form one chain. A
      system could calculate a valid test statistic, calculate a valid p-value from a
      different input, and still have two individually valid traces. The missing step
      was therefore the connection itself: prove that the exact output of the paired
      calculation is the exact input to the probability calculation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="execution-chain">
    <h2>One execution chain from observations to p-value</h2>
    <p>
      The new composition starts with the same explicit paired observations used by the
      reviewed G4 calculation. The G4 trace is verified first. The composition then
      takes the exact 64-bit representation of the resulting test statistic together
      with the integer degrees of freedom and requires them to match the Student-t tail
      trace input exactly.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a bit-level handoff, not an approximate numerical comparison. The
      composition records the G4 trace digest, the tail trace digest, the source and
      binary64 bits of the test statistic, the degrees of freedom, the tail input bits,
      and the returned p-value bits and source. Both nested traces are re-verified before
      the composition is accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The p-value returned by the composed evaluator is read from the verified tail
      trace rather than calculated again through a separate path. The result is a
      self-contained execution record for the path:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>paired observations → G4 trace → exact (t bits, df) → Student-t tail trace → p-value</code>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="review">
    <h2>What the review established</h2>
    <p>
      The independent review tested the composition with 11 successful cases spanning
      degrees of freedom 1, 2, 4, 9, 30, 149, and 200, including the exact-zero branch,
      central cases, and both signs of the series path. For every successful case, the
      directly evaluated G4 result, the directly evaluated tail result, and the composed
      result agreed on the decision-bearing trace fields and output bits.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reviewer also replaced the embedded tail trace with a different valid tail
      trace and replaced the G4 trace with a different valid G4 trace. The surrounding
      link fields and digests were then recomputed coherently. False connections were
      still rejected because the verifier reconstructs the relationship from the
      verified nested traces instead of trusting the declared digest alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Additional attacks changed all nine composition-link fields, mutated nested G4
      and tail traces while recomputing their digests, and supplied hostile object
      shapes to the validators. The reviewer reported 291 checks with zero failures and
      no blocker, should-fix, or nice-to-have finding. The full repository check also
      passed 431 tests at the reviewed implementation state.
    </p>
    <p>
      The later readiness synchronization was reviewed separately and confirmed that
      the only maturity change was recording this actual-execution composition as
      independently reviewed. The numerical logic, authority surfaces, Release 1, and
      support state did not change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>What this still does not establish</h2>
    <p>
      The composition establishes execution continuity: the paired observations, the
      G4 arithmetic trace, the exact test-statistic handoff, the Student-t tail trace,
      and the returned p-value are connected as one verifiable candidate execution. It
      does not establish that the entire chain is already bounded against the exact
      mathematical target.
    </p>
    <p>
      The G4 paired-arithmetic truth requirement and the Student-t tail numerical
      requirement have since completed independent review. See{' '}
      <a href="/engineering/bounding-paired-t-arithmetic-and-pvalues/">
        the numerical-closure engineering note
      </a>{' '}
      for those later decisions and the exact critical-value table selected for
      candidate interval work.
    </p>
    <p>
      M3-C has also connected that selected table to the paired-data trace and recorded
      the three binary64 operations used to produce the confidence-interval endpoints.
      This establishes which candidate calculation ran. It does not yet establish the
      endpoints' complete mathematical-truth bound; that remains the separate M3-D task.
    </p>
    <p>
      The work does not select supported pair, node, degree-of-freedom, value, or test-
      statistic bounds. It does not select a supported platform or execution predicate,
      supported domain, runtime path, final reason-code vocabulary, Public Check, or
      bundle. Release 2 D5 is not complete, Release 2 has not been published, and public
      review issue #25 remains open.
    </p>
    <p>
      This note follows our earlier work on{' '}
      <a href="/engineering/building-paired-t-tables-and-error-checks/">
        paired-t numerical tables and input-specific error checks
      </a>. That work focused on numerical evidence and per-input error reasoning; this
      increment focuses on connecting the actual data-to-statistic execution to the
      actual probability execution without losing provenance between them.
    </p>
  </section>

